{"qas": [
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2401.08103, how does the requirements engineering approach address trade-offs, and how is it incorporated into the proposed framework?", "answer": "The requirements engineering approach uses trade-off analysis during design and documentation, and is incorporated into the framework's proactive identification and justification steps.", "evidence": [{"page": 3, "quote": "Trade-off analysis techniques used in requirements engineering [1], [20], [28] may be applicable for determining how each ethics aspect affects an AI system under construction and for addressing the interplay between ethics aspects [25], [30]."}, {"page": 5, "quote": "The requirements engineering approach (Sec. II-C) encompasses proactive design consideration where ethics aspects, models and data types are examined, and the associated tensions and solutions are explored [30]."}]},
  {"question": "What are the three main components of the framework proposed in arXiv 2401.08103, and how do they relate to responsible AI system design?", "answer": "The three main components are proactive identification, prioritisation and weighting, and justification and documentation; they structure responsible AI system design to manage ethics trade-offs.", "evidence": [{"page": 5, "quote": "The proposed framework is comprised of three main components: (i) proactive identification, (ii) prioritisation and weighting, (iii) justification and documentation."}, {"page": 1, "quote": "we propose a framework which consists of: (i) proactive identification of tensions, (ii) prioritisation and weighting of ethics aspects, (iii) justification and documentation of trade-off decisions."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2401.08103, what are the six conditions for balancing ethics principles in the principlism approach?", "answer": "The six conditions are: stronger justification, realistic purpose, no morally preferable alternatives, minimal infringement, minimised negative effects, and impartial treatment of those affected.", "evidence": [{"page": 4, "quote": "the principlism approach provides six conditions that any balancing or trade-off must meet [5], as summarised below: 1. A stronger justification can be given for prioritising one aspect over another. 2. The purpose of overriding a given aspect has a realistic chance of being achieved. 3. There are no alternatives to this trade-off that are morally preferable. 4. The overridden aspect is infringed to the smallest extent possible to achieve the purpose of overriding it. 5. The negative effects of overriding the aspect are minimised. 6. Those affected by this trade-off are treated impartially."}]},
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2401.08103, what does the abbreviation 'AI/ML systems' refer to?", "answer": "'AI/ML systems' refers to both ML models (algorithms) and AI products.", "evidence": [{"page": 1, "quote": "In this work we use the term AI/ML systems to refer to both ML models (algorithms) and AI products."}]},
  {"question": "Which approach in arXiv 2401.08103 is described as a 'winner-takes-all' method that may result in only minimal ethical evaluation?", "answer": "The 'Dominant Aspects' approach is described as 'winner-takes-all' and consistent with ethical lip service and ethics washing.", "evidence": [{"page": 2, "quote": "A major disadvantage is that this is a winner-takes-all approach, which leaves no room to devise balanced trade-offs and take into account nuance, which in turn implies that a thorough evaluation of associated risks and benefits is not performed. This approach is hence consistent with the pejorative notions of ethical lip service [31] and ethics washing [6], where only minimal effort is expended to address ethical issues that emerge in AI/ML systems."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2401.08103, which technique is noted as the least critical for prioritising ethics aspects, and what is its main decision basis?", "answer": "The dominant aspects approach is the least critical technique, with prioritisation based on one-dimensional decisions such as difficulty or cost.", "evidence": [{"page": 5, "quote": "The least critical technique to apply is the dominant aspects approach (Sec. II-A), where prioritisation is made based on one-dimensional decisions such as difficulty or cost."}]}
]}
